it but quick decisions may indicate some. C's egocentric and communication difficulty may affect crew cooperation. B's nasty remarks also affect crew cooperation. Which is more severe? Both have issues.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. Could stay with Candidate B, or shift to C. Let's consider that stress resilience is critical for long-distance flights. B has explicit \"handles stress very well\". C lacks explicit evidence. That is a serious gap. Also B's \"very conscientious\" suggests reliability. C's reliability not explicitly stated, but Agent 1 claims 100% reliable, but we don't have that fact. So we cannot rely on that. We have no explicit reliability for C. So we might stay with B.\n\nThus we can say we remain with Candidate B, because stress resilience is proven, while C lacks evidence. Also B's technical competence (weather assessment, computer) is strong. While B's interpersonal issues are concerning, they may be mitigated with training. C's egocentric and communication issues may be more ingrained.\n\nThus we will state our recommendation remains Candidate B.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: agree/disagree with Agent 1's shift, point out missing stress resilience evidence for C, and note B's strengths.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: B very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather conditions, excellent computer skills; concerns: nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers, sometimes adopts wrong tone. C quick correct decisions, excellent attention skills, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nWe must explain why we think B still better.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON."}
